Hi!

My laptop (Dell Precision Workstation 7510) with an Intel Dual Band
Wireless-AC 8260 (see lspci and lsusb output attached), is suffering
from issues that I believe are related to the driver, or possibly the
firmware/hardware.

Specifically, when testing with Debian Sid, I am unable to connect to
2.4 GHz WiFi and Bluetooth at the same time.  I can successfully use
both independently, and 5 GHz WiFi and Bluetooth works fine.  It's been
a number of months, but I believe simultaneous 2.4 GHz and Bluetooth
worked fine in the past, and has slowly degraded.

Under Windows 10, the hardware appears to work without issue.
Additionally, I believe I managed to warm-reboot from Windows 10 to
Linux (with Bluetooth connected), and was able to continue using the
same 2.4 GHz WiFi and Bluetooth audio sink without issue.

When blueman-manager isn't hanging (it tends to do that a lot in this
situation), I am sometimes able to connect to 2.4 GHz WiFi and Bluetooth
device for a short while before the Bluetooth stack disconnects the
device.  Usually this requires removing and re-pairing the device.

After blueman-manager/the entire Bluetooth stack hangs, or if I want to
use Bluetooth and wired ethernet, I have to disconnect and disable the
WiFi interface before Bluetooth will behave like I
expect.(NetworkManager -> disconnect WiFi; # ip l set dev wlp2s0 down)

Usually at this point, the entered name for the Bluetooth host is
disregarded and the host is reported as "bluemoon universal bluetooth tool."

I would like to diagnose this, determining it as a software/firmware
issue and resolving it via supplying/testing patches, or a hardware
issue and proceed to replace it.  Let me know how I can be of
assistance; capturing bus communications from Windows and Linux or however.

Regards,
Nathan Schulte

